1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,958
Vinegar tastes sour.

2
00:00:01,958 --> 00:00:03,518
Glucose tastes sweet.

3
00:00:03,518 --> 00:00:06,376
Formalin keeps lab specimens from rotting.

4
00:00:06,776 --> 00:00:08,852
Three very different substances.

5
00:00:08,852 --> 00:00:12,379
Yet inside, their atoms
come in the very same ratio.

6
00:00:12,829 --> 00:00:15,289
Diya spots a puzzle in her kitchen.

7
00:00:15,289 --> 00:00:18,441
The glucose packet says
C six H twelve O six.

8
00:00:18,441 --> 00:00:20,342
Her notes say CH two O.

9
00:00:20,692 --> 00:00:21,974
Which one is right?

10
00:00:21,974 --> 00:00:22,874
Both are.

11
00:00:22,705 --> 00:00:25,083
They are two different kinds of formula.

12
00:00:25,583 --> 00:00:30,170
By the end of this video, you will find
a formula from nothing but percentages.

13
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:32,675
So what are these two formulas?

14
00:00:33,025 --> 00:00:37,626
The empirical formula gives the
simplest whole number ratio of atoms in

15
00:00:37,626 --> 00:00:38,606
a compound.

16
00:00:38,606 --> 00:00:41,109
Empirical means found by experiment.

17
00:00:41,509 --> 00:00:45,974
The molecular formula gives the exact
number of each kind of atom in one

18
00:00:45,974 --> 00:00:46,874
molecule.

19
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:51,325
Glucose has six carbons, twelve
hydrogens and six oxygens.

20
00:00:51,325 --> 00:00:53,594
That is its molecular formula.

21
00:00:53,994 --> 00:00:57,874
Divide every number by six,
and you get one, two, one.

22
00:00:57,874 --> 00:00:58,856
CH two O.

23
00:00:58,856 --> 00:01:00,890
That is its empirical formula.

24
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:04,022
The number we divided by is called n.

25
00:01:04,022 --> 00:01:07,770
The molecular formula is n
times the empirical formula.

26
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:10,995
Let's watch this on our molecule bench.

27
00:01:10,995 --> 00:01:12,228
Here is benzene.

28
00:01:12,578 --> 00:01:16,191
Six carbon atoms in a ring,
each holding one hydrogen.

29
00:01:16,191 --> 00:01:18,992
Every stick is a shared pair of electrons.

30
00:01:19,392 --> 00:01:20,832
Count the atoms.

31
00:01:20,832 --> 00:01:23,233
Six carbons and six hydrogens.

32
00:01:23,233 --> 00:01:24,510
C six H six.

33
00:01:24,910 --> 00:01:28,940
Now the bonds open, and we sort
the atoms into equal pieces.

34
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:31,017
Six identical pieces.

35
00:01:31,017 --> 00:01:34,279
Each piece has one
carbon and one hydrogen.

36
00:01:34,679 --> 00:01:38,845
So the empirical formula of
benzene is CH, and n is six.

37
00:01:39,345 --> 00:01:41,855
Now keep just two pieces and join them.

38
00:01:41,855 --> 00:01:45,695
Their electrons pair up again,
three pairs between the carbons.

39
00:01:46,095 --> 00:01:49,956
This is acetylene, the gas
burned in welding torches.

40
00:01:49,956 --> 00:01:51,145
C two H two.

41
00:01:51,145 --> 00:01:52,443
Here n is two.

42
00:01:53,093 --> 00:01:54,380
Pause and think.

43
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:55,982
Benzene and acetylene.

44
00:01:55,982 --> 00:01:58,776
Do they have the same empirical formula?

45
00:02:01,976 --> 00:02:02,876
Yes!

46
00:02:02,579 --> 00:02:04,352
Both reduce to CH.

47
00:02:04,352 --> 00:02:07,249
Same ratio, but different molecules.

48
00:02:07,899 --> 00:02:09,400
Now the real job.

49
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:11,874
A lab gives us only percentages.

50
00:02:11,874 --> 00:02:16,812
Glucose is forty percent carbon, six
point seven percent hydrogen, and

51
00:02:16,812 --> 00:02:19,678
fifty three point three percent oxygen.

52
00:02:20,028 --> 00:02:20,928
Step one.

53
00:02:20,895 --> 00:02:25,829
Take one hundred grams of glucose,
so each percent simply becomes grams.

54
00:02:26,579 --> 00:02:27,512
Step two.

55
00:02:27,512 --> 00:02:29,321
Turn grams into moles.

56
00:02:29,321 --> 00:02:31,425
Divide by the atomic mass.

57
00:02:31,425 --> 00:02:34,849
Carbon twelve, hydrogen
one, oxygen sixteen.

58
00:02:35,599 --> 00:02:40,017
That gives three point three three,
six point seven, and three point three

59
00:02:40,017 --> 00:02:40,923
three moles.

60
00:02:41,673 --> 00:02:42,633
Step three.

61
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:44,667
Divide each by the smallest.

62
00:02:44,667 --> 00:02:46,546
We get one, two, one.

63
00:02:46,546 --> 00:02:49,352
So the empirical formula is CH two O.

64
00:02:50,102 --> 00:02:51,099
Step four.

65
00:02:51,099 --> 00:02:51,999
Find n.

66
00:02:51,924 --> 00:02:55,407
Glucose has a molar mass
of one eighty grams.

67
00:02:55,407 --> 00:02:57,481
CH two O weighs thirty.

68
00:02:57,481 --> 00:02:58,730
So n is six.

69
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:04,921
Six times CH two O gives C six H
twelve O six, the real molecule.

70
00:03:05,571 --> 00:03:06,756
Remember this.

71
00:03:06,756 --> 00:03:09,137
Empirical is the simplest ratio.

72
00:03:09,137 --> 00:03:11,296
Molecular is the real count.

73
00:03:11,996 --> 00:03:14,877
Now, the worked example
from your NCERT book.

74
00:03:15,177 --> 00:03:20,144
A compound has four point zero seven
percent hydrogen, twenty four point

75
00:03:20,144 --> 00:03:25,444
two seven percent carbon, and seventy
one point six five percent chlorine.

76
00:03:25,444 --> 00:03:29,277
Its molar mass is ninety
eight point nine six grams.

77
00:03:29,777 --> 00:03:31,880
Divide by the atomic masses.

78
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:34,868
Hydrogen gives four point zero four moles.

79
00:03:34,868 --> 00:03:38,470
Carbon and chlorine, two
point zero two one each.

80
00:03:39,220 --> 00:03:40,983
Divide by the smallest.

81
00:03:40,983 --> 00:03:43,951
Hydrogen two, carbon one, chlorine one.

82
00:03:43,951 --> 00:03:46,790
The empirical formula is CH two CL.

83
00:03:47,540 --> 00:03:51,981
Its empirical formula mass is
forty nine point four eight grams.

84
00:03:52,731 --> 00:03:57,576
So n is ninety eight point nine six
divided by forty nine point four eight,

85
00:03:57,576 --> 00:03:58,666
which is two.

86
00:03:59,416 --> 00:04:00,842
Double everything.

87
00:04:00,842 --> 00:04:04,446
The molecular formula
is C two H four CL two.

88
00:04:05,096 --> 00:04:06,562
Now, the exam trap.

89
00:04:06,912 --> 00:04:10,297
Say your ratio comes out
as one to one point five.

90
00:04:10,297 --> 00:04:13,315
Some students round one
point five up to two.

91
00:04:13,715 --> 00:04:14,882
That is wrong.

92
00:04:14,882 --> 00:04:17,421
Multiply every number by two instead.

93
00:04:17,421 --> 00:04:20,457
One to one point five
becomes two to three.

94
00:04:21,107 --> 00:04:22,620
Where is this asked?

95
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:23,819
As a numerical.

96
00:04:23,819 --> 00:04:28,310
Percentages and a molar mass are
given, and you find both formulas.

97
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:30,240
Exam level now.

98
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:35,289
An oxide of nitrogen is thirty six
point eight percent nitrogen and sixty

99
00:04:35,289 --> 00:04:37,656
three point two percent oxygen.

100
00:04:37,656 --> 00:04:40,033
Its molar mass is seventy six.

101
00:04:40,033 --> 00:04:42,107
Find its molecular formula.

102
00:04:42,107 --> 00:04:43,493
Pause and solve.

103
00:04:48,893 --> 00:04:50,351
The answer is B.

104
00:04:50,351 --> 00:04:51,696
N two O three.

105
00:04:52,196 --> 00:04:53,236
Moles first.

106
00:04:53,236 --> 00:04:55,769
Nitrogen gives two point six three.

107
00:04:55,769 --> 00:04:58,301
Oxygen gives three point nine five.

108
00:04:58,801 --> 00:05:00,515
Divide by the smallest.

109
00:05:00,515 --> 00:05:02,239
One to one point five.

110
00:05:02,239 --> 00:05:05,364
Multiply by two, and you get two to three.

111
00:05:05,364 --> 00:05:06,827
So N two O three.

112
00:05:07,327 --> 00:05:11,003
Its mass is twenty eight plus
forty eight, seventy six.

113
00:05:11,003 --> 00:05:12,087
So n is one.

114
00:05:12,087 --> 00:05:14,958
Option A rounded one point five up to two.

115
00:05:15,558 --> 00:05:17,017
Here is your recap.

116
00:05:17,017 --> 00:05:18,517
Screenshot this card.

117
00:05:21,717 --> 00:05:24,554
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

118
00:05:24,954 --> 00:05:25,854
One.

119
00:05:25,539 --> 00:05:30,439
What is the empirical formula of
hydrogen peroxide, H two O two?

120
00:05:31,239 --> 00:05:32,139
Two.

121
00:05:31,803 --> 00:05:36,932
Why must a ratio of one to one point
five be multiplied, not rounded?

122
00:05:37,732 --> 00:05:38,632
Three.

123
00:05:38,447 --> 00:05:43,666
A compound has the empirical formula
CH two and a molar mass of fifty

124
00:05:43,666 --> 00:05:44,686
six grams.

125
00:05:44,686 --> 00:05:45,777
What is n?

126
00:05:46,677 --> 00:05:51,439
Next time: how much oxygen does
a gas stove flame really use up?

127
00:05:51,439 --> 00:05:55,101
We find out in chemical
equations and stoichiometry.
